            <fr:uri>https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0046/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>dhsorens-0046</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/dhsorens-0046/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The Ethereum Setting">The Ethereum Setting</fr:title>
            <fr:meta name="toc">true</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    The constraints that come from the deployment target rather than the mathematics. <fr:link href="/dhsorens-ethereum/" title="Ethereum" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-ethereum/" display-uri="dhsorens-ethereum" type="local">Ethereum</fr:link> wants blocks proved in seconds, on modest hardware, at a security level nobody is willing to negotiate — and increasingly with post-quantum assumptions. See <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0020/" title="Protocol Snarkification" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0020/" display-uri="dhsorens-0020" type="local">protocol snarkification</fr:link> for the broader effort, and <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0022/" title="Safely Snarkifying the Ethereum Protocol" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0022/" display-uri="dhsorens-0022" type="local">Safely Snarkifying the Ethereum Protocol</fr:link> for the talk.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/dhsorens/" title="Derek Sorensen" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens/" display-uri="dhsorens" type="local">Derek Sorensen</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>8</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:uri>https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0047/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>dhsorens-0047</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/dhsorens-0047/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Real-Time Proving and the zkEVM Target">Real-Time Proving and the zkEVM Target</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    What the protocol actually asks of a prover, stated as numbers, and which part of the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003R/" title="The zkVM Stack" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003R/" display-uri="dhsorens-003R" type="local">stack</fr:link> each number constrains.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The current latency, cost, hardware, and proof-size targets, with a source for each.</html:li>
        <html:li>Proving an L1 block: what dominates — <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0048/" title="ZK-Friendly Primitives" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0048/" display-uri="dhsorens-0048" type="local">hashing</fr:link>, signature verification, state access.</html:li>
        <html:li>Verification on the consumer side: what a home verifier must be able to check, and in what time.</html:li>
        <html:li>Multi-prover and diversity arguments, and what they assume about implementation independence.</html:li>
        <html:li>Which targets are met today and which are not.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/dhsorens/" title="Derek Sorensen" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens/" display-uri="dhsorens" type="local">Derek Sorensen</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>8</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:uri>https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0048/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>dhsorens-0048</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/dhsorens-0048/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="ZK-Friendly Primitives">ZK-Friendly Primitives</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The hash function is usually the prover's largest single cost, so primitive choice is a proof-system decision rather than an implementation detail.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    That framing is what the August 2026 decision to drop Poseidon for L1 inverts, and this page should be written with the reversal in view: <fr:link href="/dhsorens-004D/" title="Hash Functions in Bitcoin and Ethereum" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004D/" display-uri="dhsorens-004D" type="local">hash-friendly SNARKs rather than SNARK-friendly hashes</fr:link>. The cost question below is still the right question — the answer simply stopped selecting for an exotic primitive.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Arithmetisation-friendly hashes (Poseidon2 and relatives) against standard ones (Keccak, SHA-256, Blake3), counted in constraints.</html:li>
        <html:li>The security tradeoff: young algebraic designs against well-analysed bit-oriented ones.</html:li>
        <html:li>Where Ethereum has no choice — Keccak in the state trie, ECDSA in transactions — and what those cost.</html:li>
        <html:li>Hash rate as a headline benchmark, and what it does and does not predict.</html:li>
        <html:li>Which hash a <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0031/" title="Merkle Trees and Vector Commitments" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0031/" display-uri="dhsorens-0031" type="local">Merkle commitment</fr:link> should use at each layer of a recursion.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/dhsorens/" title="Derek Sorensen" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens/" display-uri="dhsorens" type="local">Derek Sorensen</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>8</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:uri>https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0049/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>dhsorens-0049</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/dhsorens-0049/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Post-Quantum Pressure">Post-Quantum Pressure</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Hash-based proof systems are plausibly post-quantum and <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0032/" title="KZG and the Pairing-Based Line" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0032/" display-uri="dhsorens-0032" type="local">pairing-based</fr:link> ones are not, which turns a cryptographic deadline into an architectural constraint on <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003P/" title="Wrapping and On-Chain Verification" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003P/" display-uri="dhsorens-003P" type="local">wrapping</fr:link>.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>What is at risk and what is not: <fr:link href="/dhsorens-004W/" title="Cyclic Groups and the Discrete Logarithm" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004W/" display-uri="dhsorens-004W" type="local">discrete-log</fr:link> commitments, hash-based commitments, the ROM analysis in a quantum setting.</html:li>
        <html:li>Hash-based signatures and aggregation, and why a SNARK is the aggregation mechanism. Which hash they are instantiated with is now <fr:link href="/dhsorens-004H/" title="Poseidon, and Why Ethereum Walked Away" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004H/" display-uri="dhsorens-004H" type="local">SHA-2 or BLAKE rather than Poseidon</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>Lattice-based commitments as an alternative, and their cost profile.</html:li>
        <html:li>The consequence for the last step of a pipeline that currently ends in Groth16.</html:li>
        <html:li>What VCV-io's lattice work already covers.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/dhsorens/" title="Derek Sorensen" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens/" display-uri="dhsorens" type="local">Derek Sorensen</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>8</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:uri>https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004A/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>dhsorens-004A</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/dhsorens-004A/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Security Levels in Production">Security Levels in Production</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p><fr:link href="/dhsorens-004N/" title="From Soundness Error to Bits of Security" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004N/" display-uri="dhsorens-004N" type="local">What bit-security a deployed system actually claims</fr:link>, under which assumptions, and who checked. This is where <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0038/" title="Soundness Regimes and the Proximity-Gap Conjectures" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0038/" display-uri="dhsorens-0038" type="local">soundness regimes</fr:link> stop being a theoretical distinction.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The parameters real provers ship: rate, repetitions, <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003A/" title="Batching and Grinding" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003A/" display-uri="dhsorens-003A" type="local">grinding</fr:link> bits, field size — and the claimed level they add up to.</html:li>
        <html:li>Which claims depend on a <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002P/" title="List Decoding and Proximity Gaps" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002P/" display-uri="dhsorens-002P" type="local">conjecture</fr:link>, and whether that is documented in the system's own materials.</html:li>
        <html:li>The accounting across <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003L/" title="IVC and Proof-Carrying Data" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003L/" display-uri="dhsorens-003L" type="local">recursion</fr:link> layers, where errors compose.</html:li>
        <html:li>Who is responsible for the number: protocol designers, implementers, or auditors.</html:li>
        <html:li>What a machine-checked parameter calculation would look like, and whether it is a reasonable thing to build.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
          </fr:mainmatter>
